\title[The Derived-Modulus Corner, Closed]{The Derived-Modulus Corner, Closed:\\
An Exact Classification of Polynomial Moduli\\ That Cannot See a Factorization}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{13 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $N=pq$ be a semiprime. A \emph{derived modulus} is an integer $M=f(N)$
obtained by evaluating a fixed integer polynomial $f$ at $N$; the canonical
examples are $N\pm 1$, $N^2+1$, the third cyclotomic value
$\Phi_3(N)=N^2+N+1$, and $2N\pm 1$. We ask whether arithmetic invariants of $M$
can carry information about the factorisation of $N$ that is not already a
function of $N$. We prove that they cannot, and we determine the exact boundary
of the obstruction.

The central identity is $N \mid f(N)-f(0)$, whence
$\gcd(N,f(N))=\gcd(N,f(0))$: the overlap between a number and its polynomial
derived modulus is \emph{frozen at the constant term}, uniformly in $N$. Calling
$f$ \emph{transparent} when $f(0)=\pm 1$, we prove that $\gcd(N,f(N))=1$ for
every integer $N$ \emph{if and only if} $f$ is transparent, with an explicit
witness $N$ whenever transparency fails. Transparent polynomials form a
multiplicative monoid stable under precomposition with any constant-term-free
polynomial, so the entire multiplicative and substitutional closure of a
transparent family remains invisible to $N$.

We add a resultant law ($\gcd(f(N),g(N)) \mid \Res(f,g)$ for all $N$), a
spectrum theorem (the primes ever dividing $N^2+1$ are exactly $2$ and those
$\equiv 1 \bmod 4$; for $\Phi_3$, exactly $3$ and those $\equiv 1 \bmod 3$ --
fixed sets of split primes independent of $N$, whence the factors of a Blum
integer are excluded by congruence), and a freshness theorem (the prime support
is infinite yet never meets $N$). All proofs use only the property
$a-b \mid F(a)-F(b)$; such maps form a subring of $\Z^{\Z}$ closed under
composition, and the barrier holds verbatim there. Outside the class it fails:
the exponential modulus $2^N-1$ does not transport congruences, shares a factor
with $N$ for infinitely many $N$, and satisfies $\gcd(253,2^{253}-1)=23$ with
$253=11\cdot 23$. The only remaining route is an external hint sharing a prime
with $N$; exactly $p+q-1$ of the $pq$ residues are useful, a density of at most
$2/B$ when both factors exceed $B$.
\end{abstract}

